+\AuthorResponse{We agree that the original description did not establish how the fixed hyperparameters had been chosen. Their initial selection rationale has not been independently established, and we do not retrospectively claim a systematic original optimization. We added a separate validation-based tuning sensitivity analysis rather than replacing the original Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:seed_stability}. XGBoost and BiLSTM-Attention each received a 27-candidate grid: seed 42 screening, rechecking the top three with seeds 42/123/7, and five-seed evaluation of the selected and original settings. The other four models received only a three-learning-rate search. Selection used validation RMSE for the point models and validation Gaussian CRPS for the three probabilistic models. The six selection files are reproducible from validation records alone; nevertheless every candidate retained test metrics, so we describe a retrospective sensitivity experiment, not a new untouched test or an equal-compute optimization.
+
+We distinguish A (original settings/cap), B (original settings/longer cap) and C (validation-selected settings/longer cap). B--A separates the epoch-cap effect, C--B parameter changes, and C--A their combination. The six-model ranking within the q19 A/C cohorts did not change across all, wet and upper-tail subsets. The BiLSTM-Attention--XGBoost overall gap narrowed from 0.001057 to 0.000161~mm\,h$^{-1}$. BiLSTM-Attention tuning slightly worsened overall RMSE, XGBoost upper-tail RMSE worsened, and Transformer improved numerically after extending its training cap. Only BU-LSTM-Attention CRPS improvement met the stated three-part rule: five improving seeds and both pointwise intervals below zero. This does not constitute multiple-comparison control. We preserve negative results, both interval types and their disagreements. q19 uses q21/q14 neural A-reference reruns; in particular BU-LSTM-Attention and Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention are not the complete original Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:seed_stability} run collections. The original no-attention BiLSTM was not retuned. We make no claim that validation-selected settings are universally optimal or that all architectures received equal search budgets.
+
+The archive contains 117 unique q19 runs and matching prediction records. Their accumulated recorded task time is 3.87~h, including construction, fitting and validation/test prediction through the timer stop. It is neither pure fit time nor GPU-hours, and excludes earlier A-reference training. XGBoost accounted for 39 records and 16.4~s, BiLSTM-Attention 38 records and approximately 1.31~h; the light searches had 11 records each for Transformer, BU-LSTM and BU-LSTM-Attention, and seven for Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention. The host records identify a visible RTX 5090 and TensorFlow 2.21.0, but do not establish whether XGBoost executed on CPU or GPU. Separate inference timing was not recorded. Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_search} reports the search spaces, original/selected configurations, full validation rankings, run counts and recorded costs; Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_effects} gives all saved A/B/C effects, improving-seed counts, seed and paired-bootstrap intervals, and the three-part rule. The 28-group BiLSTM line in the statistics log includes the cap-30 gate run; the actual search contains 27 cap-60 candidates, as reported in the table.
+
+The proposed complete repository address is \begingroup\def\UrlBreaks{\do\/\do-\do.}\url{https://github.com/Liilychee/2026---Earth-Science-Informatics---Regional-Precipitation-Forecasting-under-review-}\endgroup. Its final publication scope, reproducibility documentation and final release commit are explicitly marked pending in the manuscript Code Availability statement, to be completed after the full revision. We do not claim that a finalized README/environment package has already been published. The current scientific q19 evidence is independently pinned to archived commit \texttt{d02519c5f153feb712018cf97efb035f6ca2d7ae}; this experimental-source identifier is not presented as the final code release. This repository-publication item remains open. Methods and Results (Sections~\ref{manuscript-sec:experimental_setup} and~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_results}), Discussion and Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations} now explain the sensitivity analysis and its limits.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Added paragraphs in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:experimental_setup}, Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:overall_results}, Discussion and Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}; Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_search} and~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_effects}; Code Availability pending final repository release. Final page and line numbers remain to be supplied after the complete revision.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Validation selection and unequal budgets}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The original comparison used the fixed configurations documented in Table~\ref{tab:model_config}; their initial selection rationale could not be independently established, and we do not claim an original systematic search. A separate revision sensitivity analysis used validation-based selection. XGBoost searched 27 combinations of tree count, learning rate and depth; BiLSTM-Attention searched 27 combinations of recurrent units, learning rate and dropout. Seed 42 screened each grid, the top three were rechecked with seeds 42, 123 and 7, and the selected and original configurations were evaluated with five seeds. Transformer and the three Gaussian recurrent models instead received only a three-learning-rate search at seed 42, selecting by validation RMSE for Transformer and full-validation Gaussian CRPS for the probabilistic models. Every candidate retained test metrics, but the archived selection rules use validation scores only. The search spaces and budgets are not equal across models (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_search}).}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{A/B/C definitions and pointwise uncertainty}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Within this sensitivity analysis, A denotes the original settings and epoch cap, B the original settings with a longer cap, and C the validation-selected settings with that longer cap. BiLSTM-Attention used caps 30 and 60; the other neural models used 30 and 100, with early-stopping patience 10. XGBoost has A=B. The A references for the neural models are q21/q14 reruns, not a replacement for the complete original Table~\ref{tab:seed_stability} run set. Paired differences compare seed-level scores, using the same five seeds and test targets. Separate pointwise 95\% seed-$t$ and moving-block percentile intervals use four degrees of freedom and 28-sample blocks, 5,000 replicates and resampling seed 42, respectively. Sampled indices are truncated to the test length before applying rainfall masks; within-seed score differences are then averaged, rather than averaging predictions first. A descriptive three-way improvement criterion requires all five seeds to improve and both intervals to lie below zero; it is not a multiplicity correction (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_effects}).}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Sensitivity results and run-set boundary}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{In the q19 sensitivity cohort, the six-model RMSE ordering was unchanged between A and C for the full, revised-wet and upper-tail test subsets. The overall BiLSTM-Attention advantage over XGBoost nevertheless narrowed from 0.001057 to 0.000161~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Validation-selected BiLSTM-Attention had slightly higher overall RMSE (+0.06\%), while XGBoost upper-tail RMSE increased by 0.26\%, with no seed showing improvement. Transformer improved numerically by 0.78\% from A to B and a further 0.59\% from B to C, giving 1.37\% overall; its seed interval still included zero. Only BU-LSTM-Attention CRPS improvement met the three-way rule (1.73\% lower). These rankings concern the q19 A/C collections: in A, BU-LSTM precedes BU-LSTM-Attention, unlike their order in the original Table~\ref{tab:seed_stability}. Both interval types and unfavourable effects are retained in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_effects}.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Recorded task-time scope}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The 117 unique q19 task records sum to 3.87~h. Their timer covers model construction, fitting and validation/test prediction up to its stop; it is not pure fit time, an end-to-end search wall-clock measurement or GPU-hours. The XGBoost execution device and separate inference cost were not recorded. This total excludes the earlier q21/q14 A-reference runs. Per-model counts, recorded task times and validation candidate rankings are given in Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_search}.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Retrospective and model-selection limitations}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The q19 search is a retrospective sensitivity analysis on one existing chronological split, with unequal search spaces and different validation selection objectives. Candidate test scores were stored, so validation-only selection does not turn this into a new, untouched model-development test. The intervals are pointwise and unadjusted for multiple comparisons. Search uncertainty and computational cost do not exhaust model-design uncertainty, and the conclusions are restricted to the tested configurations, data split and regional target.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Code Availability: explicit unfinished release item}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Proposed code-and-evidence repository (final publication scope and version pending): \begingroup\def\UrlBreaks{\do\/\do-\do.}\url{https://github.com/Liilychee/2026---Earth-Science-Informatics---Regional-Precipitation-Forecasting-under-review-}\endgroup. \textbf{[CODE-AVAILABILITY TODO: confirm the final public link and release commit, review the publication scope, and complete the repository documentation after all reviewer revisions.]} The current archive is not represented here as a finalized, fully documented reproduction package.}
+}
+\endgroup
 
