 {\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red,filecolor=red,urlcolor=red}The main-comparison training protocol below describes the original deterministic and Gaussian models. The alternative-likelihood reruns retain that training budget but use the distinct output heads, losses and target scales defined in Section~\ref{sec:probabilistic_output}.\par}
 
 The deep-learning models were implemented in TensorFlow/Keras and trained using the Adam optimizer. Unless otherwise specified by the model architecture, the learning rate was set to 0.001, the batch size to 32, and the maximum number of training epochs to 30. Validation loss was monitored during training, and early stopping was applied when no improvement was observed for ten consecutive epochs. The model weights associated with the lowest validation loss were restored before evaluation. Deterministic neural networks were trained using mean squared error, whereas the probabilistic models were optimized using Gaussian negative log-likelihood. Hyperparameter settings were fixed before evaluation on the test set.
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red,filecolor=red,urlcolor=red}
+The original comparison used the fixed configurations documented in Table~\ref{tab:model_config}; their initial selection rationale could not be independently established, and we do not claim an original systematic search. A separate revision sensitivity analysis used validation-based selection. XGBoost searched 27 combinations of tree count, learning rate and depth; BiLSTM-Attention searched 27 combinations of recurrent units, learning rate and dropout. Seed 42 screened each grid, the top three were rechecked with seeds 42, 123 and 7, and the selected and original configurations were evaluated with five seeds. Transformer and the three Gaussian recurrent models instead received only a three-learning-rate search at seed 42, selecting by validation RMSE for Transformer and full-validation Gaussian CRPS for the probabilistic models. Every candidate retained test metrics, but the archived selection rules use validation scores only. The search spaces and budgets are not equal across models (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_search}).
+
+Within this sensitivity analysis, A denotes the original settings and epoch cap, B the original settings with a longer cap, and C the validation-selected settings with that longer cap. BiLSTM-Attention used caps 30 and 60; the other neural models used 30 and 100, with early-stopping patience 10. XGBoost has A=B. The A references for the neural models are q21/q14 reruns, not a replacement for the complete original Table~\ref{tab:seed_stability} run set. Paired differences compare seed-level scores, using the same five seeds and test targets. Separate pointwise 95\% seed-$t$ and moving-block percentile intervals use four degrees of freedom and 28-sample blocks, 5,000 replicates and resampling seed 42, respectively. Sampled indices are truncated to the test length before applying rainfall masks; within-seed score differences are then averaged, rather than averaging predictions first. A descriptive three-way improvement criterion requires all five seeds to improve and both intervals to lie below zero; it is not a multiplicity correction (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m9_effects}).
+\par}
